development.solution_text:
$\arcsin x$ exists only for $-1\le x\le 1$, and sine then undoes it, so $y=x$ on that interval and the function is undefined elsewhere.
The graph is the line segment $y=x$ from $(-1,-1)$ to $(1,1)$, with both endpoints included.
Domain: $-1\le x\le 1$. Range: $-1\le y\le 1$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=1.7]
\draw[->] (-1.7,0) -- (1.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-1.7) -- (0,1.8) node[above] {$y$};
\draw (1,0.06) -- (1,-0.06) node[below, font=\small] {$1$};
\draw (-1,0.06) -- (-1,-0.06) node[below, font=\small] {$-1$};
\draw (0.06,1) -- (-0.06,1) node[left, font=\small] {$1$};
\draw (0.06,-1) -- (-0.06,-1) node[left, font=\small] {$-1$};
\draw[thick] (-1,-1) -- (1,1) node[pos=0.85, below right, font=\small] {$y=\sin(\arcsin x)$};
\fill (-1,-1) circle (1.2pt);
\fill (1,1) circle (1.2pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

mastery.solution_text:
For $0\le x\le\pi$ the composite is $y=x$.
For $\pi\le x\le 2\pi$ the angle with the same cosine inside $[0,\pi]$ is $2\pi-x$, so $y=2\pi-x$.
Cosine is even, so the graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis, giving $y=-x$ on $[-\pi,0]$ and $y=x+2\pi$ on $[-2\pi,-\pi]$.
The graph is a triangular wave with peaks of height $\pi$ at $x=\pm\pi$ and zeros at $x=0$ and $x=\pm 2\pi$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, x=0.9cm, y=0.9cm]
\draw[->] (-4.7,0) -- (4.9,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.7) -- (0,3.1) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \x/\lab in {-4/{$-2\pi$}, -2/{$-\pi$}, 2/{$\pi$}, 4/{$2\pi$}} \draw (\x,0.08) -- (\x,-0.08) node[below, font=\small] {\lab};
\draw (0.08,1) -- (-0.08,1) node[left, font=\small] {$\frac{\pi}{2}$};
\draw (0.08,2) -- (-0.08,2) node[left, font=\small] {$\pi$};
\draw[thick, <->] (-4,0) -- (-2,2) -- (0,0) -- (2,2) node[above right, font=\small] {$y=\arccos(\cos x)$} -- (4,0);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
